VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-006
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe self-injurious behaviour and physical aggression.
Location: Group residential home
Present at Visit: Patient and two support workers
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of recurrent head-banging against walls and physical aggression towards staff, rendering the environment unsafe. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the high risk of permanent self-inflicted traumatic brain injury and the immediate need for chemical restraint and medical evaluation to exclude an underlying acute painful trigger.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The patient is continuously smashing their head against the window and biting anyone who approaches.
History of Present Illness: Support staff report a 3-hour history of escalating severe destructive behaviour targeting the environment and physical aggression directed towards caregivers. The episode began with stereotypic repetitive hand-wringing and vocalizations following an unexpected change in the evening meal routine. This rapidly progressed into recurrent self-directed physical harm, specifically severe head-banging and self-biting. The patient has profound autism spectrum disorder and profound difficulty communicating pain or basic needs. The intensity of the episode has escalated continuously over the past 60 minutes despite standard de-escalation protocols.
Functional Status: Ambulant; requires 1:1 supervision for activities of daily living.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of severe self-harm and staff injury; high risk of elopement.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75.0 Kg
General: Patient is highly agitated, pacing, and exhibiting loud, non-verbal vocalizations. Forehead shows fresh contusions.
Physical Exam: Neurological and physical exam limited by severe agitation. Observation confirms active self-injurious acts and physical aggression when staff approach. The patient displays profoundly impaired communication, relying entirely on distress vocalizations. The current episode is linked to recognizable environmental triggers (routine disruption). Examination of the head reveals a 4x4cm boggy hematoma on the frontal bone without active bleeding. Abdomen appears soft upon visual inspection during pacing; no obvious dental abscess visible from a distance.
Environmental Observations: Broken glass from a shattered picture frame on the floor; doors are locked to prevent absconding.
Medication Reconciliation: PRN Olanzapine available but staff were unable to safely administer it orally.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Behaviours of concern (e.g.Self injury, aggression) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Undiagnosed Dental Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of facial swelling or localized holding of the jaw, and the clear temporal link to a routine disruption.

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever or specific ear-tugging behavior.

Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to normal voiding patterns observed today and absence of foul-smelling urine or fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of intractable self-injury and physical aggression represents a psychiatric and physical emergency. The objective findings of a frontal hematoma and continuous severe agitation driven by impaired communication confirm that the patient is actively sustaining physical trauma. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because immediate medical intervention was required to safely administer chemical de-escalation, rule out an acute physical pain trigger, and prevent catastrophic neurological injury from ongoing head-banging.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular Olanzapine 10mg to achieve acute sedation and prevent further self-harm. Applied ice pack to the frontal hematoma once sedated.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you introduce the visual schedule board 30 minutes before any transition, the patient anticipates the change; therefore, utilizing the board consistently for every routine shift reduces the anxiety that triggers aggression. When you reinforce calm periods immediately with a preferred sensory toy, the patient links calm behavior with positive input; doing this frequently outside of crisis moments builds emotional regulation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the complex behavior support team for a functional behavioral assessment review.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended installation of impact-absorbing wall padding in the patient's bedroom.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow to assess sedation effects and hematoma.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 for police and ambulance support if the patient wakes up and resumes severe head-banging, if the patient becomes unarousable, or if vomiting and unequal pupils develop indicating a severe head injury.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The acute agitation is managed symptomatically; underlying behavioral drivers require specialized functional assessment.
Clinical Course: Sedation is expected to last 6-8 hours, providing a window to reset the environment and investigate for any hidden sources of pain.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Staff must monitor the patient every 15 minutes while sedated. Seek immediate emergency care if signs of concussion (vomiting, uneven pupils, inability to wake) appear.

